{"name":"Kimberly","hex":"#695d87","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Kimberly","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A surname.","A unisex given name transferred from the surname, popular in the 1960s and the 1970s."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Kimberly"}],"entries":[{"title":"Kimberley, Queensland","blocks":[{"text":"Kimberley is a coastal locality in the Shire of Douglas, Queensland, Australia. In the 2021 census, Kimberley had a population of 28 people."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4e/Kimberley_Valley%2C_Far_North_Queensland.JPG/330px-Kimberley_Valley%2C_Far_North_Queensland.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kimberley%2C_Queensland"},{"title":"County of Kimberley","blocks":[{"text":"County of Kimberley is a cadastral unit located in the Australian state of South Australia \nthat covers land to the east of the Flinders Ranges about 45 kilometres (28 mi) east of the town of Peterborough. It was proclaimed in 1871 and named after John Wodehouse, 1st Earl of Kimberley, a British Secretary of State for the Colonies. It has been partially divided in the following sub-units of hundreds – Gumbowie, Hardy, Ketchowla, Parnaroo, Terowie and Wonna."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Old_wooden_shop_Terowie.JPG/330px-Old_wooden_shop_Terowie.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/County_of_Kimberley"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/34/Hundred_of_Gumbowie%2C_1889_%2822743350280%29.jpg/120px-Hundred_of_Gumbowie%2C_1889_%2822743350280%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/34/Hundred_of_Gumbowie%2C_1889_%2822743350280%29.jpg/250px-Hundred_of_Gumbowie%2C_1889_%2822743350